From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winepulse: send DualShock 4 audio to and from Bluetooth controllers.

A Bluetooth DualShock 4 has no USB audio function, so games find no
speaker or headset microphone endpoint for it. When winebus presents the
controller as USB (Steam Input disabled), expose synthetic endpoints like
the wired ones, with the same container ID, and bridge them through hidraw,
paced in real time, using libsbc loaded at runtime:

- Speaker (stereo 32 kHz): four SBC frames (32 kHz dual channel, 16 blocks,
  8 subbands, loudness, bitpool 25, 112 bytes each) per 16 ms output report
  #0x17. Streams of any rate are resampled. The headset jack state from the
  input reports selects the speaker or headset output, and the volumes are
  set with output report #0x11.
- Headset microphone (mono 16 kHz): with a headset microphone plugged in,
  the controller sends one input report #0x12 to #0x19 with an SBC frame
  (16 kHz mono, bitpool 29, 66 bytes, 8 ms) per output report it receives,
  so while it is captured a no-op output report is sent every 8 ms without
  another one. Frames are decoded into a ring, and capture streams read it
  with a 48 ms jitter reserve, resampled to their rate.

This reuses the Bluetooth DualSense mixer, so several streams on the
speaker are mixed on one clock. PROTON_DUALSHOCK4_BT_AUDIO=0 disables it.

Packet layouts and codec settings follow DS4Dongle
(https://github.com/snipem/DS4Dongle), whose audio code is MPL-2.0.
